nd concealed weapons and the anthem that our neighborhoods are not safe because of the black males roaming our streets.\nWe can continue to also deny the subtle, subconscious, unconscious, call it what you will, inability to see racial stereotyping, racial profiling and all the other biases in our nation against persons of color.\nUntil we have an honest dialogue with ourselves, and then with our neighbors, about our feelings, fears and biases regarding race, we will continue to see tragedies like the Martin youth losing his life.\nGeorge Zimmerman’s fears were exacerbated by the prior crime in his neighborhood, as well as the prevalent societal assumptions regarding black males. I don’t doubt he thought he was acting valiantly on behalf of the neighborhood.\nNeighborhood Watch associations, however, are not supposed to condone or promote vigilante actions. He called the police, but then ignored their directive.\nYears ago, those fears might have ended up in a fist fight. Today, sadly, with laws promoting gun ownership and self-defense, one man’s misguided need to take inappropriate action ended up killing a young man.\nZimmerman’s life will also never be the same, nor will his family’s. What a terrible price to pay for actions that should have been the purview of the police and only the police.\nGiven the fact that senseless deaths occur every day in our communities, it seems to give us a sense of being helpless in this onslaught. However, we can take action as citizens.\nJoin an action group in favor of better and more sensible laws regarding gun ownership. Write your state and congressional representatives.\nAnd finally, volunteer to mentor young people if you have the time.\nWhat if Zimmerman wasn’t carrying gun?\nI wonder what George Zimmerman would have done if he was not in the possession of a loaded gun.\nZimmerman would probably have stayed in his vehicle and not approached Trayvon Martin face to face, causing the lost of a life.\nWhen we approach someone in a threatening manner, we must expect that person to defend himself. Zimmerman approached Martin, which caused the confrontation and death.\nStand your ground should have made Zimmerman stay in his vehicle. No one was trying to remove him from the vehicle.\nNarcissism flourishing in Savannah\nI have encountered a lot of people in my 43 years of being here, but none can compare to the narcissistic individuals who I try to relate to and who don’t relate to me.\nThere seems to be a very large number of the","tokens":["Nothing",","," in"," my"," mind",","," screams"," out"," for"," gun"," control"," and"," a"," change"," in"," laws"," like"," Florida","’","s"," “","Stand"," Your"," Ground","”"," legislation"," like"," the"," tragic"," death"," of"," T","ray","von"," Martin",","," a"," young"," 17","-","year","-","old"," male"," returning"," home"," from"," a"," convenience"," store"," in"," his"," Florida"," neighborhood",".","\n","Regardless"," of"," where"," each"," of"," us"," are"," emotionally"," in"," the"," not"," guilty"," verdict",","," this"," tragedy"," reflects"," the"," continuing",","," day","-","by","-","day"," sens","eless"," deaths"," on"," our"," streets",".","\n","We"," can"," blame"," it"," on"," the"," black","-","on","-","black"," crime"," in"," our"," cities",","," the"," need"," to"," protect"," ourselves"," with"," guns"," and"," concealed"," weapons"," and"," the"," anthem"," that"," our"," neighborhoods"," are"," not"," safe"," because"," of"," the"," black"," males"," ro","aming"," our"," streets",".","\n","We"," can"," continue"," to"," also"," deny"," the"," subtle",","," sub","conscious",","," unconscious",","," call"," it"," what"," you"," will",","," inability"," to"," see"," racial"," stere","otyping",","," racial"," profiling"," and"," all"," the"," other"," biases"," in"," our"," nation"," against"," persons"," of"," color",".","\n","Until"," we"," have"," an"," honest"," dialogue"," with"," ourselves",","," and"," then"," with"," ou